<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=maya-script">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Writing and counting</p><h1 class="enc-title">Maya script</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">3rd century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a> · Also known as Maya glyphs, Maya hieroglyphs</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Maya script, or Maya glyphs, is the writing system of the Maya civilisation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesoamerica">Mesoamerica</a>, known from inscriptions of the 3rd century BCE at late Preclassic sites such as San Bartolo in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala">Guatemala</a> and Chakjobon in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>. It combined logograms with syllabic signs and was painted on pottery, walls and bark-paper codices or carved in stone and wood. Used until the Spanish conquest, it is the only Mesoamerican script substantially deciphered: over 90 per cent of texts can now be read with reasonable accuracy, after work by Yuri Knorozov and others.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/maya-script.jpg" alt="Maya script" width="320" height="223" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Kwamikagami · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Palenque_glyphs-edit1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The script was used mainly in the Classic period to write Classic Maya, a literary form of the extinct Chʼoltiʼ language, on stelae and monuments. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/classic-maya-collapse">Classic Maya collapse</a> its use declined and disappeared in the central and southern lowlands. In the Postclassic period it continued in northern Yucatán to write Yucatec Maya, in the surviving codices such as the Dresde, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrid">Madrid</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>. Not every Mayan language used it: the Quichean languages, such as Kʼicheʼ and Kaqchikel, used the Mixteca-Puebla style of central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>.</p><p>It was a logosyllabic system. A glyph could stand for a word or morpheme or for a syllable. Syllabic glyphs often came from logograms for one-syllable words, so that the sign for a fish fin, read kah, came to stand for the syllable ka. The word bʼalam, jaguar, could be written with one logogram, with syllabic additions, or wholly in syllables as bʼa-la-ma. Texts were arranged in blocks, usually in columns two blocks wide.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The earliest inscriptions identifiably Maya date to the 3rd century BCE, at late Preclassic sites such as Chakjobon and San Bartolo. It was once thought that the Maya took writing from the Olmec or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epi-olmec-culture">Epi-Olmec culture</a>, but murals excavated in 2005 pushed the origin back by several centuries, and it now seems possible that the Maya invented writing in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesoamerica">Mesoamerica</a>. Knowledge of the script was kept to the elite.</p><p>Bishop <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diego-de-landa">Diego de Landa</a> ordered Maya books collected and destroyed, and many codices were lost. He also recorded a so-called alphabet of Maya signs, which was long dismissed as nonsense but eventually became a key resource in decipherment. Knowledge of the script was lost, probably by the end of the 16th century.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>Nineteenth-century and early twentieth-century scholars decoded the numbers and passages on astronomy and the calendar. In 1952 Yuri Knorozov argued that Landa's signs were syllabic, and J. Eric S. Thompson was among the last major opponents of this approach. In 1959 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatiana-proskouriakoff">Tatiana Proskouriakoff</a> showed that the dates on the monuments at Piedras Negras recorded the lives of individuals, so that the inscriptions were dynastic histories. In December 1973 a working group at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palenque">Palenque</a>, including Linda Schele, Floyd Lounsbury and Peter Mathews, reconstructed most of the dynastic list of Palenque in one afternoon.</p><p>Since the early 1980s scholars have shown that most of the remaining signs form a syllabary, and the exhibition The Blood of Kings in 1986 brought the results to a wide audience.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Maya script” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Mesoamerica was the only region of the Americas where full writing systems developed, and the <b>Maya script</b>, which combined signs for words and syllables, could record any sentence of the Maya languages.</blockquote><a href="/religion/mesoamerican-religion">Mesoamerican religion · Texts &amp; Teachers</a></li><li><blockquote>At San Bartolo, murals painted around 100 BCE show the maize god and the crowning of a king, and texts of the 3rd century BCE found there are among the earliest <b>Maya writing</b>.</blockquote><a href="/culture/maya-civilisation">Maya civilisation · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>It is written in <b>Maya hieroglyphs</b> and is thought to copy an earlier text.</blockquote><a href="/work/dresden-codex">Dresden Codex · How It Was Made</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>3rd century BCE</dd></div><div><dt>Where</dt><dd>San Bartolo (Guatemala) and Chakjobon (Mexico)</dd></div><div><dt>Credited to</dt><dd><a href="/culture/maya-civilisation">Maya civilisation</a></dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>It was long thought that the Maya took writing from the Olmec or Epi-Olmec; murals found in 2005 make an independent invention possible.</p><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=maya-script">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
